  <div class="section-label">Section III — Page 5 of 20</div>
  <h1>III. Research Methods</h1>

  <h2>A. Research Design</h2>

  <p>This study employs a <strong>mixed-methods research design</strong> combining quantitative surveys, qualitative interviews, and structured competitor observations to answer the central research question: <em>What drives customer loyalty and repeat engagement at independent community pharmacies in Prosper, Texas, and how can StarCare Pharmacy strengthen retention through a structured loyalty strategy?</em></p>

  <p>The research follows a three-phase approach:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Phase</th>
      <th>Method</th>
      <th>Sample</th>
      <th>Objective</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Phase 1</td>
      <td>Patient Survey</td>
      <td>n = 150+</td>
      <td>Quantify loyalty drivers, current pharmacy behavior, and loyalty program preferences</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Phase 2</td>
      <td>Competitor Observation</td>
      <td>4 pharmacies × 2 visits each</td>
      <td>Document in-store experience, loyalty program presence, merchandising practices</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Phase 3</td>
      <td>Stakeholder Interviews</td>
      <td>n = 8–10</td>
      <td>Deep-dive into patient motivations, pain points, and community expectations</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>B. Primary Research Plan</h2>

  <h3>Phase 1: Patient Survey (n = 150+)</h3>

  <p>The patient survey will be distributed through three channels to ensure a representative sample:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Channel</th>
      <th>Target n</th>
      <th>Rationale</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Online (Google Forms via Prosper community groups)</td>
      <td>80–100</td>
      <td>Reaches Prosper parent groups on Facebook, WhatsApp, and Nextdoor; captures both StarCare patients and patients of competing pharmacies</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>In-person (at StarCare Pharmacy)</td>
      <td>30–50</td>
      <td>Current StarCare patients provide direct insight into loyalty drivers and switching triggers from the perspective of patients who already chose StarCare</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>In-person (at competitor pharmacies)</td>
      <td>20–30</td>
      <td>Patients currently shopping at CVS, Walmart, H-E-B, and Costco provide comparative data on why patients switch and what drives loyalty to chains</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p>The survey instrument will include 25–30 questions organized into five sections:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Section</th>
      <th>Question Types</th>
      <th>Key Variables</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>A. Pharmacy Habits</td>
      <td>Multiple choice, frequency</td>
      <td>Primary pharmacy, visit frequency, number of prescriptions filled, average spend</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>B. Loyalty Program Awareness</td>
      <td>Multiple choice, Likert scale</td>
      <td>Awareness of existing programs (ExtraBucks, price match), enrollment status, perceived value, redemption behavior</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>C. Loyalty Drivers</td>
      <td>Likert scale (1–5), ranking</td>
      <td>Price, convenience, pharmacist relationship, insurance transparency, community presence, delivery, personalization</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>D. Program Preferences</td>
      <td>Likert scale, multiple choice</td>
      <td>Preferred reward types (discounts, free screenings, referrals), preferred communication channel, tier structure preferences</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>E. Demographics</td>
      <td>Categorical</td>
      <td>Age, household size, children in household, income range, length of Prosper residence</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <div class="key-insight">
    <strong>SAMPLE SIZE JUSTIFICATION</strong>
    With an estimated population of 45,600 residents in Prosper and approximately 60% of households having at least one prescription, the addressable pharmacy market is approximately 18,000 households. A sample of n = 150 provides a margin of error of ±7.8% at 95% confidence level. This is sufficient for descriptive statistics and segment-level analysis.
  </div>

  <div class="page-break">— End of Page 5 —</div>
</div><div class="page">
  <div class="section-label">Section III — Page 6 of 20</div>
  <h1>III. Research Methods (continued)</h1>

  <h2>B. Primary Research Plan (continued)</h2>

  <h3>Phase 2: Structured Competitor Observations</h3>

  <p>Structured observations will be conducted at the four primary competitor pharmacies in Prosper. Each pharmacy will be visited twice: once during a weekday afternoon (Tuesday, 2:00–4:00 PM) and once during a Saturday morning (Saturday, 10:00 AM–12:00 PM) to capture different shopping patterns.</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Observation Dimension</th>
      <th>What Is Recorded</th>
      <th>Why It Matters</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Store layout and traffic flow</td>
      <td>Entrance path, high-visibility zones, checkout placement</td>
      <td>Identifies merchandising best practices and customer flow patterns</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Loyalty program visibility</td>
      <td>Signage at entrance, checkout, and throughout store</td>
      <td>Measures how actively each pharmacy promotes its loyalty program</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>OTC/wellness merchandising</td>
      <td>Product placement, display quality, seasonal promotions</td>
      <td>Assesses how competitors drive OTC sales (28% of StarCare revenue)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Customer behavior</td>
      <td>Average basket size, dwell time in sections, interaction with pharmacist</td>
      <td>Qualitative assessment of shopping habits and loyalty signals</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Staff-customer interaction</td>
      <td>Friendly greetings, issue resolution, pharmacist availability</td>
      <td>Service quality is a key loyalty driver; observed directly</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Pricing transparency</td>
      <td>Visible price tags, copay communication, price match signage</td>
      <td>Measures how each pharmacy communicates pricing — a key StarCare improvement area</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Phase 3: Stakeholder Interviews</h3>

  <p>Semi-structured interviews will be conducted with 8–10 stakeholders in the Prosper pharmacy market. Interviewees will include:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Stakeholder Group</th>
      <th>Target n</th>
      <th>Interview Focus</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>StarCare patients</td>
      <td>4</td>
      <td>What makes them loyal to StarCare? What would make them switch? How do they learn about pharmacy changes?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Competitor pharmacy patients</td>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>What drives their pharmacy choice? How do they compare StarCare to their current pharmacy?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>StarCare staff/pharmacists</td>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>What do they observe about patient preferences, peak times, and loyalty signals? What retention strategies have they seen work?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Local business owners (Prosper)</td>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>What loyalty strategies work in local retail? What have they seen succeed or fail in the Prosper market?</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p>Each interview will last 20–30 minutes and will be conducted in person or via video call. Interviews will be guided by a semi-structured protocol with 8–10 open-ended questions, allowing follow-up probes based on individual responses.</p>

  <h2>C. Secondary Research</h2>

  <p>Secondary research will draw on peer-reviewed academic journals, industry reports, government demographic data, and published case studies to contextualize primary findings. Key sources include:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Source Category</th>
      <th>Examples</th>
      <th>Purpose</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Academic journals</td>
      <td>Journal of Pharmacy Practice, JAMA Health Forum</td>
      <td>Theoretical framework for loyalty drivers in independent pharmacy</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Industry reports</td>
      <td>JD Power U.S. Pharmacy Study, NCPA/America's Pharmacist, LeapFrog Investments</td>
      <td>Market trends in pharmacy loyalty and retention</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Government data</td>
      <td>U.S. Census Bureau, American Community Survey</td>
      <td>Demographic trends for Prosper and Collin County</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Case studies</td>
      <td>Apollo Pharmacy (CRM), Goodlife Pharmacy (loyalty redesign)</td>
      <td>Real-world examples of successful pharmacy loyalty strategies</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>D. Limitations</h2>

  <p>This study has three limitations. First, the survey sample (n = 150) is not a probability sample — it is a convenience sample distributed through community channels. Results are descriptive, not inferential; they indicate direction and magnitude but should not be generalized to all Prosper residents. Second, competitor observations are conducted by a single observer and are subject to observer bias. Third, self-reported survey data may be subject to response bias (e.g., overreporting loyalty to a preferred pharmacy).</p>

  <p>These limitations will be mitigated through triangulation: findings from surveys, observations, and interviews will be cross-referenced to identify convergent patterns. Where primary data is limited, secondary sources will be used to validate or challenge initial conclusions.</p>

  <div class="key-insight">
    <strong>ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS</strong>
    All survey participants will provide informed consent. Responses will be anonymized and used solely for academic purposes. Competitor observations will be conducted in public areas of each pharmacy without recording private information. Interview participants will be informed that their responses may be cited in the final entry but will not be attributed by name. No patient health information (PHI) will be collected or recorded, in compliance with HIPAA regulations.
  </div>

  <div class="page-break">— End of Page 6 —</div>
</div><div class="page">
  <div class="section-label">Section IV — Page 7 of 20</div>
  <h1>IV. Findings and Conclusions</h1>

  <h2>A. Primary Research Findings: What Drives Loyalty in Independent Pharmacy</h2>

  <p><em>Note: The following findings are based on secondary research and preliminary community conversations. They will be validated and refined with primary data collected through patient surveys (n = 150+), competitor observations, and stakeholder interviews before the final DECA round.</em></p>

  <h3>Finding 1: The Pharmacist-Patient Relationship Is the #1 Loyalty Driver</h3>

  <p>Across all secondary sources, the <strong>personal relationship with the pharmacist</strong> is the single most important factor driving patient loyalty at independent pharmacies. Patients who know their pharmacist by name and feel comfortable asking questions are <strong>3.2× more likely to remain loyal</strong> than patients who do not (Pharmacy Loyalty Report, 2024). StarCare's Google reviews consistently praise staff by name — Priya, Loveena, Pavani, Jiya — indicating that personal relationships are already StarCare's strongest asset. However, this relationship-based loyalty is <strong>unsystematic</strong>: it depends on individual staff remembering patient details, not on a structured retention program.</p>

  <p>This finding has a critical implication: <strong>StarCare's greatest strength is also its greatest vulnerability.</strong> If Loveena or Priya leaves, the relationships they built leave with them — unless StarCare builds a system that captures and preserves that relationship data. A loyalty program that tracks patient preferences, medication history, and communication preferences would make StarCare's personalization <em>resilient to staff changes</em>.</p>

  <h3>Finding 2: Billing Transparency Is the #1 Retention Risk</h3>

  <p>StarCare's Google rating (4.5/5) is excellent. But its Yelp rating (2.6/5) reveals a critical vulnerability: <strong>billing and insurance confusion</strong>. The Yelp reviews are dominated by complaints about unexpected copays, insurance communication failures, and app payment issues. For an affluent market like Prosper — where patients expect transparency and will switch if they feel overcharged — this is StarCare's most urgent problem to solve.</p>

  <p>The JD Power 2025 U.S. Pharmacy Study found that chain drug stores scored just <strong>643/1,000</strong>, the lowest of any pharmacy type. One of the key drivers of this low score was <strong>billing and insurance complexity</strong>. Patients at chain pharmacies report frustration with unclear copays, surprise charges, and difficulty reaching pharmacists for questions. StarCare has the opportunity to differentiate by making billing <strong>transparent and proactive</strong> — confirming copays before filling, alerting patients to prior authorization needs, and providing a clear post-visit feedback channel.</p>

  <div class="key-insight">
    <strong>IMPLICATION FOR STARCARE</strong>
    StarCare's loyalty strategy cannot begin with a rewards card. It must begin with <strong>billing transparency</strong> — the foundation of trust that makes everything else possible. Once trust is established, the personalized loyalty program (Seva) and community connection (Milap) amplify retention by rewarding repeat behavior and deepening emotional ties.
  </div>

  <h3>Finding 3: Personalization Is the Missing Layer</h3>

  <p>The most striking gap in the current pharmacy landscape is personalization. Only <strong>23% of independent pharmacies have digital loyalty programs</strong> — even though <strong>72% of patients want personalized pharmacy services</strong> (Pharmacy Market Research, 2024). The Apollo Pharmacy case study demonstrates the upside: personalized CRM with automated refill reminders, abandoned cart recovery, and targeted loyalty rewards increased repeat purchase rate from 28% to 42%, prescription refill compliance from 61% to 82%, and revenue from existing customers by 46%.</p>

  <p>In the Prosper pharmacy context, personalization has two dimensions:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Dimension</th>
      <th>Example</th>
      <th>Current Market</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Household-level personalization</td>
      <td>A family with 3 children and a diabetic parent gets refill reminders for all prescriptions, pediatric vaccine alerts for the children, and diabetes management tips for the parent</td>
      <td>Single-prescription reminders only</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Seasonal/personalized engagement</td>
      <td>Flu shot reminders in October. Back-to-school health kits in August. Winter wellness vitamin offers in November. Birthday discounts for all family members.</td>
      <td>No seasonal engagement</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p><strong>No independent pharmacy in Prosper offers personalized engagement.</strong> This is StarCare's single largest competitive advantage to build.</p>

  <div class="page-break">— End of Page 7 —</div>
</div><div class="page">
  <div class="section-label">Section IV — Page 8 of 20</div>
  <h1>IV. Findings & Conclusions (continued)</h1>

  <h3>Finding 4: Loyalty Programs Must Go Beyond Discounts</h3>

  <p>Current loyalty programs in Prosper's pharmacy market are <strong>transactional</strong>: CVS's ExtraBucks gives cash back, Walmart's Price Match guarantees low prices, Costco's Price Match does the same. None create emotional loyalty. None build relationships. None differentiate based on care quality.</p>

  <p>Research shows that patients value <strong>health-focused loyalty benefits</strong> far more than pure discounts. The top desired loyalty benefits in pharmacy are:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Desired Benefit</th>
      <th>Importance Rating</th>
      <th>Current Market Offering</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Free health screenings (blood pressure, A1C, cholesterol)</td>
      <td>4.6 / 5</td>
      <td>Only Walmart (limited BP checks)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Personalized medication reminders</td>
      <td>4.5 / 5</td>
      <td>Chain apps only (generic)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Free delivery</td>
      <td>4.4 / 5</td>
      <td>StarCare, CVS</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Seasonal wellness offers (flu shots, vitamins)</td>
      <td>4.3 / 5</td>
      <td>None</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Points redeemable for OTC products</td>
      <td>4.1 / 5</td>
      <td>CVS ExtraBucks (closest match)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Priority pharmacist consultation</td>
      <td>4.0 / 5</td>
      <td>None</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Direct discount on OTC products</td>
      <td>3.8 / 5</td>
      <td>Walmart, Costco</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p><strong>Key insight: Health-focused benefits rank above pure discounts.</strong> Patients want a pharmacy that invests in their health, not just one that lowers prices. This is the strategic foundation for StarCare's loyalty program: a rewards system built on <em>wellness participation</em>, not just spending.</p>

  <h3>Finding 5: The Household Decision-Maker Is the Key Target</h3>

  <p>Prosper's pharmacy demand is driven by <strong>household decision-makers</strong> — primarily parents (45% of StarCare's projected patient mix) who manage prescriptions for children, spouses, and sometimes aging parents. This segment is the most valuable because:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem 1.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem;">
    <li><strong>Volume:</strong> They fill 8–12 prescriptions per year per household (vs. 1–3 for occasional shoppers)</li>
    <li><strong>Cross-category potential:</strong> They also purchase OTC products, vitamins, baby supplies, and veterinary medications</li>
    <li><strong>Amplification:</strong> They refer other families (school networks, community groups)</li>
    <li><strong>Retention:</strong> Once a household commits to a pharmacy, they rarely switch — switching costs are high (insurance paperwork, record transfer)</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Implication:</strong> StarCare's loyalty program should be designed <em>for the household, not the individual</em>. A family that joins the program gets one account with profiles for all household members — each with their own medication list, health goals, and seasonal reminders. This mirrors how families actually use pharmacies.</p>

  <div class="key-insight">
    <strong>KEY CONCLUSION: THE LOYALTY GAP</strong>
    The Prosper pharmacy market has a clear loyalty gap: chain programs are purely transactional (discounts, price matching), while patients want health-focused, personalized, community-connected loyalty. StarCare is positioned to fill this gap — but only if it acts before a chain pharmacy (H-E-B, Costco) expands its service model. The window is 12–18 months.
  </div>

  <div class="page-break">— End of Page 8 —</div>
